410→                           A problem of Erdős and Rosenfeld. This is trivially possible for $k=2$. They were not sure about $k=6$.<br><br>This is equivalent to asking whether there exists $k&gt;2$ such that the chromatic number of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Johnson_graph" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">Johnson graph</a> $J(2k,k)$ is $k+1$ (it is always at least $k+1$ and at most $2k$). The chromatic numbers listed at <a href="https://www.win.tue.nl/~aeb/graphs/Johnson.html" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">this website</a> show that this is false for $3\leq k\leq 8$. <br><br>Ma and Tang <a href="https://github.com/QuanyuTang/erdos-problem-835/blob/main/On_Problem_835.pdf" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer ugc" target="_blank">have proved</a> that the chromatic number of $J(2k,k)$ is $&gt;k+1$ for all $k&gt;2$ not of the form $p-1$ for prime $p$.<br>
